            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Recognition and Impact</strong><br>\nCarlos Chagas's work was revolutionary and brought him many accolades, including nominations for the Nobel Prize in Medicine. His discovery had a significant impact on public health in Brazil and other tropical countries, where Chagas disease remains a major health concern. His research contributed to the broader understanding of tropical diseases.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Contributions to Medicine</strong><br>\nBeyond discovering Chagas disease, Carlos Chagas made substantial contributions to the study of malaria, leishmaniasis, and other tropical diseases. He implemented public health measures to control these diseases, which improved the lives of countless individuals in rural and poverty-stricken areas.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Oswaldo Cruz Institute</strong><br>\nAt the Oswaldo Cruz Institute, Chagas succeeded his mentor, Oswaldo Cruz, as director in 1917. Under his leadership, the institute became a world-renowned center for research in tropical medicine. He also established a network of research outposts throughout Brazil to study and combat endemic diseases.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
