          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Public Health Initiatives</strong><br>\nChagas was a pioneer in public health. He understood the relationship between social conditions and disease and advocated for improving living standards as a way to combat health problems. He worked to educate the public about hygiene and health, which was as crucial as his scientific research.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Legacy</strong><br>\nCarlos Chagas married in 1903 and was a devoted family man, often taking his children with him on his research trips. He died on November 8, 1934, leaving behind a legacy of scientific curiosity and dedication to improving public health. His name is honored by the Carlos Chagas Institute of Postgraduate Medical Studies.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Continuing Fight Against Chagas Disease</strong><br>\nToday, Chagas disease affects millions of people, primarily in Latin America. Efforts to control the disease's spread continue, based on the groundwork laid by Carlos Chagas. His discovery not only unveiled a hidden medical challenge but also set the stage for ongoing research and interventions against vector-borne diseases.</p>\n"
              },
